{"name":"Grand Rapids","hex":"#38707e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Grand Rapids","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A place in the United States:\nA city, the county seat of Kent County, Michigan.\n\nA city, the county seat of Itasca County, Minnesota.\nAn unincorporated community in LaMoure County, North Dakota.\nA village and township in Wood County, Ohio.\nA town in Wood County, Wisconsin. The city of Wisconsin Rapids was also named Grand Rapids, but was renamed in 1920.","A city, the county seat of Kent County, Michigan."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Grand_Rapids"}],"entries":[{"title":"Grand Rapids, Michigan","blocks":[{"text":"Grand Rapids is a city in and the county seat of Kent County, Michigan. The city had a population of 198,917 at the 2020 census and an estimated population of 201,183 in 2025. Grand Rapids is the second-most populated municipality in the state after Detroit. It is the central city of the Grand Rapids metropolitan area, which has a population of around 1.2 million and is the 49th-largest metropolitan area in the United States."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Meyer_May_House.jpg/330px-Meyer_May_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Rapids%2C_Michigan","wikidata":"Q184587"},{"title":"Rapids","blocks":[{"text":"Rapids are sections of a river where the river bed has a relatively steep gradient, causing an increase in water velocity and turbulence. Flow, gradient, constriction, and obstacles are four factors that are needed for a rapid to be created."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Rapids_of_Kern_River%2C_California%2C_USA.webm/330px--Rapids_of_Kern_River%2C_California%2C_USA.webm.jpg","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rapids","wikidata":"Q695793"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thu